{0 Transform hierarchy and entity lifecycle}

The engine ships two optional, independent systems for spatial hierarchy and
controlled entity destruction. Use one, both, or neither — they share a command
bus but have no module dependency on each other.

{table
  {tr {th System} {th Responsibility}}
  {tr {td {!Eon_engine.Transform_system}} {td DFS world-transform propagation; [`Reparent] command handling}}
  {tr {td {!Eon_engine.Lifecycle_system}} {td [`Destroy_entity] command; guards with [World.is_alive]}}
}

{1 Components}

Four components make up the hierarchy. All are registered by
[Components.Engine_components.register_all].

{table
  {tr {th Component} {th Written by} {th Read by}}
  {tr {td [Local_transform]} {td Game code, physics sync} {td [Transform_system] (read during DFS)}}
  {tr {td [World_transform]} {td [Transform_system] only} {td Rendering, spatial audio, game queries}}
  {tr {td [Parent]}          {td [Transform_system] via [`Reparent]} {td [Transform_system] (DFS traversal)}}
  {tr {td [Children]}        {td [Transform_system] via [`Reparent]} {td [Transform_system] (DFS traversal)}}
}

[Local_transform] is the component game code writes. [World_transform] is the
derived, read-only result. Never write [World_transform] or [Children] directly —
[Transform_system] owns both.

{[
  open Eon_engine

  (* Local_transform — position/rotation/scale relative to parent (or world origin) *)
  type local : Components.Local_transform.t = {
    position : Math.Vec2.t;
    rotation : float;       (* radians *)
    scale    : Math.Vec2.t;
  }

  (* World_transform — fully composed world-space transform; same fields, read-only *)
]}

{1 Setting up}

{2 Register components}

[Components.Engine_components.register_all] registers all engine components
including the four hierarchy components:

{[
  open Eon_engine

  let world = World.create ()
  let ()    = Components.Engine_components.register_all world
]}

{2 Create systems and wire the pipeline}

Both systems follow the [Make(Sys : System.DISPATCH)] / [Default] pattern.
[Default] is pre-applied with [System.Default] — use it for the common case:

{[
  open Eon_engine

  (* Create system instances *)
  let lifecycle  = Lifecycle_system.Default.make ()
  let transforms = Transform_system.Default.make ()

  (* IMPORTANT: register Transform_system BEFORE Lifecycle_system.
     Single_bus dispatches in pipeline registration order (FIFO),
     so Transform_system's handler runs first — detaching children
     before Lifecycle_system removes the entity. *)
  let pipeline =
    Pipeline.Default.create ()
    |> Pipeline.Default.add_phase `Lifecycle
    |> Pipeline.Default.add_system `Lifecycle lifecycle
    |> Pipeline.Default.add_phase `Transform
    |> Pipeline.Default.add_system `Transform transforms
    (* `Transform must come after phases that write Local_transform
       and before phases that read World_transform *)
]}

{1 Building a transform hierarchy}

{2 Spawn entities with local transforms}

{[
  open Eon_engine

  let world = World.create ()
  let ()    = Components.Engine_components.register_all world

  (* Spawn a character at world position (100, 200) *)
  let character = World.create_entity world
  let () =
    World.set_component world character Components.Local_transform.component
      { position = Math.Vec2.create 100.0 200.0;
        rotation = 0.0;
        scale    = Math.Vec2.one }

  (* Spawn a weapon at offset (20, 0) — relative to whatever parent it gets *)
  let weapon = World.create_entity world
  let () =
    World.set_component world weapon Components.Local_transform.component
      { position = Math.Vec2.create 20.0 0.0;
        rotation = 0.0;
        scale    = Math.Vec2.one }
]}

{2 Attach a child to a parent}

Hierarchy mutations during simulation go through the [`Reparent] command.
[Transform_system] processes it during [drain] — keeping [Parent] and
[Children] consistent — then propagates world transforms during the next
[update]:

{[
  open Eon_engine

  (* Attach weapon to character *)
  Single_bus.emit (Buses.Default.commands ())
    (`Reparent Transform_hierarchy.{ entity = weapon; new_parent = Some character })
]}

After [Transform_system.update] runs:

- [character.World_transform] = [character.Local_transform] (it is a root)
- [weapon.World_transform.position] = [(120, 200)] — character world position
  + weapon local offset

{2 Attach at setup time}

[Transform_hierarchy.attach] is a synchronous helper for world configuration before
the simulation loop starts. It writes [Parent] and [Children] directly —
no command bus required:

{[
  open Eon_engine

  let world = World.create ()
  let ()    = Components.Engine_components.register_all world

  let root   = World.create_entity world
  let torso  = World.create_entity world
  let weapon = World.create_entity world

  (* Build the hierarchy before the loop *)
  let () = Transform_hierarchy.attach world ~parent:root   ~child:torso
  let () = Transform_hierarchy.attach world ~parent:torso  ~child:weapon

  (* root → torso → weapon — Transform_system will propagate on the first frame *)
]}

{b Only for setup time.} Once the simulation loop is running, use [`Reparent]
commands instead — [Transform_system] needs to see the mutation during drain to
keep [Parent] and [Children] consistent. Calling [Transform_hierarchy.attach] mid-simulation
bypasses that and will leave the hierarchy in an inconsistent state.

{2 Detach a child}

Pass [new_parent = None] to detach and make an entity a root again:

{[
  Single_bus.emit (Buses.Default.commands ())
    (`Reparent Transform_hierarchy.{ entity = weapon; new_parent = None })
]}

[weapon.Parent] is removed. [character.Children] is updated (the entry is
removed; the [Children] component is dropped entirely if no children remain).
[weapon] retains its last [World_transform] until the next [update].

{2 How world transforms compose}

[Transform_system] uses a DFS walk from root entities (entities with
[Local_transform] and no [Parent]):

{[
  (* Composition formula *)
  (* world.position = parent_world.position
       + rotate(parent_world.scale * child_local.position, parent_world.rotation)
     world.rotation = parent_world.rotation + child_local.rotation
     world.scale    = parent_world.scale * child_local.scale   (* component-wise *) *)
]}

One frame of latency: [Reparent] commands emitted during frame N's tick are
processed during frame N's drain. Frame N+1's [update] sees the new tree.

{b Non-uniform scale:} a parent with [scale = (2.0, 1.0)] stretches child
position offsets along the X axis before rotation is applied. This is
mathematically correct but can be unintuitive. Avoid non-uniform scale on
hierarchy parents unless the distortion is intentional.

{1 Destroying entities}

{2 With Lifecycle_system}

Emit [`Destroy_entity] instead of calling [World.destroy_entity] directly.
[Transform_system] detaches children first; [Lifecycle_system] removes the
entity last:

{[
  open Eon_engine

  (* Safe: Transform_system detaches children, then Lifecycle_system destroys *)
  Single_bus.emit (Buses.Default.commands ()) (`Destroy_entity character)
]}

After [drain]:

- [character] is removed from the world
- [weapon] is still alive; its [Parent] component has been removed — it is
  now a root entity with its last known [World_transform]
- Emitting [`Destroy_entity] twice for the same entity is safe — [Lifecycle_system]
  guards with [World.is_alive]

{2 Cascade destruction with despawn_recursive}

[Transform_hierarchy.despawn_recursive] walks the subtree depth-first and emits
[`Destroy_entity] for every node — deepest descendants first, the root
entity last. Call it from a system's [update] (tick phase); the commands
are queued and processed during drain as usual:

{[
  open Eon_engine

  (* In a Parallel system's update — reads world (ro), emits commands *)
  let update (world : World.ro World.t) _dt =
    let emit = Single_bus.emit (Buses.Default.commands ()) in
    (* despawn the full subtree rooted at `root_entity` *)
    Transform_hierarchy.despawn_recursive world root_entity ~emit
]}

[Transform_system] detaches children and [Lifecycle_system] calls
[World.destroy_entity] — both fire during drain in pipeline registration
order. No manual traversal required.

For a flat entity with no children the call is equivalent to emitting a
single [`Destroy_entity].

{2 Cascade destruction (manual)}

If you prefer an explicit one-level cascade without [Transform_hierarchy.despawn_recursive]:

{[
  (* Shallow cascade: immediate children only *)
  let destroy_with_children world entity =
    (match World.get_component world entity Components.Children.component with
     | None -> ()
     | Some { entities } ->
       List.iter (fun child ->
         Single_bus.emit (Buses.Default.commands ()) (`Destroy_entity child)
       ) entities);
    Single_bus.emit (Buses.Default.commands ()) (`Destroy_entity entity)
]}

{2 Without Lifecycle_system}

If [Lifecycle_system] is not registered, call [World.destroy_entity] directly
and clean up the hierarchy manually before doing so:

{[
  open Eon_engine

  let destroy_manual world entity =
    (* Detach children first so they don't hold stale Parent references *)
    (match World.get_component world entity Components.Children.component with
     | None -> ()
     | Some { entities } ->
       List.iter (fun child ->
         World.remove_component world child Components.Parent.component
       ) entities;
       World.remove_component world entity Components.Children.component);
    World.destroy_entity world entity
]}

[Transform_system] is unaware — it sees only live entities in its next DFS pass.

{1 Using only Transform_system}

[Lifecycle_system] is fully optional. If your game never needs [`Destroy_entity]
commands — because it calls [World.destroy_entity] directly, or entities are
never destroyed — simply omit [Lifecycle_system] from the pipeline:

{[
  let pipeline =
    Pipeline.Default.create ()
    |> Pipeline.Default.add_phase `Transform
    |> Pipeline.Default.add_system `Transform (Transform_system.Default.make ())
]}

{1 Using only Lifecycle_system}

[Lifecycle_system] works without [Transform_system]. [`Destroy_entity] commands
are processed and [World.destroy_entity] is called — no hierarchy cleanup
happens, but that's fine if entities have no hierarchy components:

{[
  let pipeline =
    Pipeline.Default.create ()
    |> Pipeline.Default.add_phase `Lifecycle
    |> Pipeline.Default.add_system `Lifecycle (Lifecycle_system.Default.make ())
]}

{1 Extending Lifecycle_system}

[Lifecycle_system.Default.make] accepts an optional [~on_command] callback
that fires {b before} the built-in [`Destroy_entity] handler — the entity is
still alive when it runs, so all components are accessible:

{[
  open Eon_engine

  let lifecycle = Lifecycle_system.Default.make
    ~on_command:(fun world cmd ->
      match cmd with
      | `Destroy_entity entity ->
        (* entity still alive here — read components before destruction *)
        (match World.get_component world entity Components.Local_transform.component with
         | Some lt -> Log.info "destroying entity at %.1f %.1f" lt.position.x lt.position.y
         | None -> ());
        Audio.stop_all world entity
      | _ -> ()
    )
    ()
]}

The callback receives the {b full command type} — not just [`Destroy_entity].
This makes [Lifecycle_system] an extensible lifecycle hub: spawn logic, audio
teardown, logging, particle despawn — all in one place, with correct ordering
relative to [Transform_system]:

{[
  open Eon_engine

  let lifecycle = Lifecycle_system.Default.make
    ~on_command:(fun world cmd ->
      match cmd with
      | `Spawn { position; prefab } -> Prefab.instantiate world ~position prefab
      | `Destroy_entity entity      -> Fx.play_death_effect world entity
      | _ -> ()
    )
    ()
]}

For complex cases — a dedicated audio system, a particle system with its own
state — a separate handler-only system registered before [Lifecycle_system]
remains the right approach. The two compose cleanly.

{1 Custom DISPATCH pipeline}

Both systems expose [Make(Sys : System.DISPATCH)] for use with a non-default
pipeline. [Default] is simply [Make(System.Default)]:

{[
  open Eon_engine

  (* Wire into a custom pipeline with a domain-pool executor *)
  module My_pipeline = Pipeline.Make
    (System.Make(Eon_ecs.System.Make(Signals)(Events)(Commands)))
    (Executor.Domain_pool.Make(struct let size = 4 end))
    (Buses.Default)

  module My_dispatch = System.Make(Eon_ecs.System.Make(Signals)(Events)(Commands))

  let transforms = Transform_system.Make(My_dispatch).make ()
  let lifecycle  = Lifecycle_system.Make(My_dispatch).make ()
]}

{1 What stays in game code}

[Transform_system] and [Lifecycle_system] provide the machinery. Game code provides
the domain logic:

- {b Movement systems} — write [Local_transform] to move or rotate entities
- {b Physics sync} — rigidbody position → [Local_transform] before [Transform_phase];
  read [World_transform] for queries after
- {b Rendering} — read [World_transform] for draw-call positions
- {b Camera follow} — read [World_transform] of the target entity, adjust viewport
- {b Spawning hierarchies} — create entities, set [Local_transform], emit [`Reparent]
- {b Cascade destruction} — emit [`Destroy_entity] for each child, then the parent
